[v8 152.2.0] v8_target_cpu
[v8 152.2.0]     Current value (from the default) = ""
[v8 152.2.0]       From //build/config/v8_target_cpu.gni:33
[v8 152.2.0] 
[v8 152.2.0]     This arg is used when we want to tell the JIT-generating v8 code
[v8 152.2.0]     that we want to have it generate for an architecture that is different
[v8 152.2.0]     than the architecture that v8 will actually run on; we then run the
[v8 152.2.0]     code under an emulator. For example, we might run v8 on x86, but
[v8 152.2.0]     generate arm code and run that under emulation.
[v8 152.2.0]    
[v8 152.2.0]     This arg is defined here rather than in the v8 project because we want
[v8 152.2.0]     some of the common architecture-specific args (like arm_float_abi or
[v8 152.2.0]     mips_arch_variant) to be set to their defaults either if the current_cpu
[v8 152.2.0]     applies *or* if the v8_current_cpu applies.
[v8 152.2.0]    
[v8 152.2.0]     As described below, you can also specify the v8_target_cpu to use
[v8 152.2.0]     indirectly by specifying a `custom_toolchain` that contains v8_$cpu in the
[v8 152.2.0]     name after the normal toolchain.
[v8 152.2.0]    
[v8 152.2.0]     For example, `gn gen --args="custom_toolchain=...:clang_x64_v8_arm64"`
[v8 152.2.0]     is equivalent to setting --args=`v8_target_cpu="arm64"`. Setting
[v8 152.2.0]     `custom_toolchain` is more verbose but makes the toolchain that is
[v8 152.2.0]     (effectively) being used explicit.
[v8 152.2.0]    
[v8 152.2.0]     v8_target_cpu can only be used to target one architecture in a build,
[v8 152.2.0]     so if you wish to build multiple copies of v8 that are targeting
[v8 152.2.0]     different architectures, you will need to do something more
[v8 152.2.0]     complicated involving multiple toolchains along the lines of
[v8 152.2.0]     custom_toolchain, above.
[v8 152.2.0] 
